1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,720
Every day, your body makes huge numbers of

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:06,000
new cells by mitosis, and each time DNA

3
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:09,360
is copied, a few changes can slip through.

4
00:00:09,480 --> 00:00:11,840
So most of us carry cells with changed

5
00:00:11,920 --> 00:00:12,760
DNA.

6
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:15,920
Yet most of those cells never become cancer.

7
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:16,960
Why not?

8
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:19,760
And when one does, how does it spread?

9
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:23,280
That's today's question: how does cancer begin and

10
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:24,000
spread?

11
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:26,720
Start with what you know.

12
00:00:27,280 --> 00:00:30,680
Before mitosis, a cell copies its DNA.

13
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:34,880
Mitosis separates the chromosome copies into two nuclei.

14
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:38,440
A cancer cell still divides by mitosis.

15
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:41,280
The main problem isn't how it divides, but

16
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:42,160
when.

17
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:44,960
Healthy cells divide when the body needs them,

18
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:46,640
like when a graze heals.

19
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:49,360
A cancer cell keeps dividing when it should

20
00:00:49,440 --> 00:00:50,000
stop.

21
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:53,280
A cell's life runs in a loop called

22
00:00:53,320 --> 00:00:54,520
the cell cycle.

23
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:58,240
It grows, copies its DNA, gets ready, then

24
00:00:58,320 --> 00:00:59,040
divides.

25
00:00:59,680 --> 00:01:02,600
Along the loop sit checkpoints, stops where the

26
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:04,720
cell checks that it's safe to go on.

27
00:01:04,960 --> 00:01:07,680
You've met one: before the copies separate, the

28
00:01:07,760 --> 00:01:10,400
cell waits until every chromosome is attached to

29
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:12,080
fibres from both poles.

30
00:01:12,720 --> 00:01:15,040
Another comes before the DNA is copied.

31
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:17,920
It asks: is the cell big enough, is

32
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:20,240
there a signal to divide, and is the

33
00:01:20,320 --> 00:01:21,840
DNA undamaged?

34
00:01:23,067 --> 00:01:27,146
At that checkpoint, a protein called p53 responds

35
00:01:27,227 --> 00:01:28,506
to DNA damage.

36
00:01:28,747 --> 00:01:31,466
It stops the cycle, so repair enzymes can

37
00:01:31,547 --> 00:01:32,986
fix the damage.

38
00:01:33,067 --> 00:01:36,026
If the damage is too great, p53 triggers

39
00:01:36,107 --> 00:01:39,786
apoptosis: the cell destroys itself in a controlled

40
00:01:39,867 --> 00:01:40,506
way.

41
00:01:40,827 --> 00:01:43,546
And many mutations that do slip through don't

42
00:01:43,587 --> 00:01:45,706
affect the genes that control division.

43
00:01:45,947 --> 00:01:47,066
That's the first answer.

44
00:01:47,627 --> 00:01:50,586
Cancer usually needs several changes that defeat those

45
00:01:50,667 --> 00:01:51,546
controls.

46
00:01:52,767 --> 00:01:55,166
Those changes usually build up in the same

47
00:01:55,247 --> 00:01:56,606
line of cells.

48
00:01:56,687 --> 00:01:58,446
A cell with one change that lets it

49
00:01:58,527 --> 00:02:01,166
divide a bit more often leaves more descendants

50
00:02:01,247 --> 00:02:02,606
than its neighbours.

51
00:02:02,687 --> 00:02:04,846
One of those picks up a second change,

52
00:02:04,967 --> 00:02:07,406
and its descendants take over again.

53
00:02:07,487 --> 00:02:09,886
That's why it usually takes years, and why

54
00:02:09,967 --> 00:02:12,526
cancer becomes more common as people age.

55
00:02:13,734 --> 00:02:14,773
Which genes?

56
00:02:15,014 --> 00:02:16,613
Two kinds control division.

57
00:02:17,094 --> 00:02:20,133
Proto-oncogenes are normal genes that tell a cell

58
00:02:20,214 --> 00:02:22,493
to divide when a growth signal arrives.

59
00:02:22,694 --> 00:02:23,893
They're the accelerator.

60
00:02:24,374 --> 00:02:27,333
A mutation can jam the accelerator down, so

61
00:02:27,374 --> 00:02:30,213
the gene's protein says divide even with no

62
00:02:30,294 --> 00:02:31,493
signal from outside.

63
00:02:31,974 --> 00:02:34,533
The mutated gene is called an oncogene.

64
00:02:34,934 --> 00:02:37,573
And one mutated copy is enough, because a

65
00:02:37,774 --> 00:02:40,773
protein that's stuck on overrides the normal copy.

66
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:45,040
The other kind are tumour suppressor genes, the

67
00:02:45,120 --> 00:02:45,760
brakes.

68
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:50,640
They stop division, repair DNA or trigger apoptosis,

69
00:02:50,720 --> 00:02:52,880
and the gene for p53 is one of

70
00:02:52,960 --> 00:02:53,200
them.

71
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:57,200
One working copy usually still makes enough brake,

72
00:02:57,320 --> 00:02:59,680
so both copies normally have to be lost.

73
00:03:00,240 --> 00:03:02,800
That's why inheriting one faulty copy, like a

74
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:06,400
faulty BRCA1 gene, raises the risk of cancer

75
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:08,640
a lot, but doesn't make it certain.

76
00:03:09,360 --> 00:03:12,160
So cancer is a stuck accelerator and failed

77
00:03:12,240 --> 00:03:15,920
brakes, though real cancers need several such changes.

78
00:03:17,134 --> 00:03:18,333
Now a real case.

79
00:03:18,414 --> 00:03:21,613
Australia has some of the world's highest melanoma

80
00:03:21,814 --> 00:03:24,173
rates, and UV light from the sun is

81
00:03:24,254 --> 00:03:25,293
its main cause.

82
00:03:25,934 --> 00:03:29,533
UV damages DNA in melanocytes, the skin cells

83
00:03:29,574 --> 00:03:30,733
that make pigment.

84
00:03:31,054 --> 00:03:34,653
In many melanomas, one mutation jams an accelerator

85
00:03:34,734 --> 00:03:38,653
gene called BRAF, and later mutations knock out

86
00:03:38,734 --> 00:03:39,573
brakes.

87
00:03:39,934 --> 00:03:42,333
That cell line divides out of control and

88
00:03:42,414 --> 00:03:44,733
forms a tumour, a lump of cells that

89
00:03:44,894 --> 00:03:45,853
keep dividing.

90
00:03:47,067 --> 00:03:49,066
Compare that with a benign mole.

91
00:03:49,467 --> 00:03:52,346
A benign tumour, like most moles, stays where

92
00:03:52,427 --> 00:03:53,146
it started.

93
00:03:53,547 --> 00:03:56,066
It can grow, but it doesn't invade.

94
00:03:56,347 --> 00:03:59,386
A malignant tumour, like our melanoma, does.

95
00:03:59,867 --> 00:04:02,186
Its cells break through a thin sheet under

96
00:04:02,267 --> 00:04:04,266
the top layer of skin, called the basement

97
00:04:04,347 --> 00:04:07,386
membrane, into the layer below, where blood and

98
00:04:07,467 --> 00:04:08,746
lymph vessels run.

99
00:04:09,067 --> 00:04:12,346
A way to remember it: malignant moves, benign

100
00:04:12,427 --> 00:04:13,306
is bound.

101
00:04:14,534 --> 00:04:17,813
Spreading to a new site is called metastasis,

102
00:04:17,894 --> 00:04:19,733
and it takes several steps.

103
00:04:20,054 --> 00:04:22,773
Cells break away, because they've lost the proteins

104
00:04:22,854 --> 00:04:24,573
that stick them to their neighbours.

105
00:04:24,854 --> 00:04:27,173
They squeeze into a lymph or blood vessel

106
00:04:27,414 --> 00:04:28,453
and get carried off.

107
00:04:29,014 --> 00:04:30,653
Most die on the way.

108
00:04:30,934 --> 00:04:33,733
A few survive, leave the vessel somewhere else

109
00:04:33,894 --> 00:04:35,893
and grow a new, secondary tumour.

110
00:04:36,454 --> 00:04:39,253
For melanoma, that's often a nearby lymph node

111
00:04:39,334 --> 00:04:42,373
first, then organs such as the lungs, liver

112
00:04:42,614 --> 00:04:43,413
or brain.

113
00:04:44,634 --> 00:04:47,673
That's why metastasis causes most cancer deaths.

114
00:04:48,234 --> 00:04:50,953
Once tumours grow in several organs, they're much

115
00:04:51,034 --> 00:04:52,073
harder to remove.

116
00:04:52,634 --> 00:04:56,233
It's also why finding melanoma early matters: while

117
00:04:56,314 --> 00:04:58,953
it's thin and hasn't reached the vessels, cutting

118
00:04:59,034 --> 00:05:01,193
it out is often successful.

119
00:05:01,274 --> 00:05:04,073
And the main cause can be reduced: slip,

120
00:05:04,181 --> 00:05:06,633
slop, slap, seek and slide.

121
00:05:07,834 --> 00:05:09,553
Two exam questions.

122
00:05:09,834 --> 00:05:10,313
First.

123
00:05:10,954 --> 00:05:13,673
Cell A has an activating mutation that keeps

124
00:05:13,834 --> 00:05:16,873
one copy of a proto-oncogene switched on.

125
00:05:17,194 --> 00:05:19,593
Cell B has a mutation that stops one

126
00:05:19,674 --> 00:05:22,233
copy of a tumour suppressor gene working, and

127
00:05:22,314 --> 00:05:24,633
one normal copy is enough for that gene's

128
00:05:24,714 --> 00:05:25,033
job.

129
00:05:25,514 --> 00:05:27,353
Every other copy is normal.

130
00:05:27,674 --> 00:05:30,153
Predict which cell is more likely to divide

131
00:05:30,354 --> 00:05:32,953
without a growth signal, and explain your prediction.

132
00:05:33,514 --> 00:05:34,793
Pause here to answer.

133
00:05:45,200 --> 00:05:46,000
Cell A.

134
00:05:46,480 --> 00:05:50,080
Its activating mutation makes an oncogene, whose protein

135
00:05:50,160 --> 00:05:52,720
is stuck on, so one copy can drive

136
00:05:52,960 --> 00:05:54,880
division with no signal.

137
00:05:54,960 --> 00:05:57,080
In cell B, the normal copy of the

138
00:05:57,120 --> 00:06:00,080
tumour suppressor gene still makes enough brake, so

139
00:06:00,160 --> 00:06:03,520
control holds until that copy is lost too.

140
00:06:03,600 --> 00:06:06,160
The classic mistake is saying both are equally

141
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:08,800
likely, because each has one mutation.

142
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:10,480
Second.

143
00:06:11,040 --> 00:06:15,280
Some types of human papillomavirus, or HPV, infect

144
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:17,360
cells lining the cervix.

145
00:06:17,440 --> 00:06:20,880
One viral protein, called E6, causes the cell

146
00:06:20,920 --> 00:06:23,280
to destroy its p53.

147
00:06:23,360 --> 00:06:25,840
Explain how this infection can increase the risk

148
00:06:25,920 --> 00:06:27,200
of cancer.

149
00:06:27,360 --> 00:06:29,920
Then explain why a growth that hasn't crossed

150
00:06:29,960 --> 00:06:33,280
the basement membrane is unlikely to have spread.

151
00:06:33,440 --> 00:06:34,640
Pause here to answer.

152
00:06:45,067 --> 00:06:49,386
Losing p53 weakens a key DNA-damage checkpoint.

153
00:06:49,947 --> 00:06:52,826
More damaged cells can survive and divide, so

154
00:06:53,147 --> 00:06:54,986
mutations can build up in that line.

155
00:06:55,547 --> 00:06:58,266
That raises the risk, but not every infection

156
00:06:58,347 --> 00:06:59,306
leads to cancer.

157
00:06:59,787 --> 00:07:02,026
And to spread, cells must break through the

158
00:07:02,107 --> 00:07:04,106
basement membrane to reach vessels.

159
00:07:04,667 --> 00:07:07,546
The classic mistake is calling cervical cancer a

160
00:07:07,627 --> 00:07:08,986
cancer you catch.

161
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:13,720
So for any cancer question, follow the chain:

162
00:07:14,040 --> 00:07:17,400
mutations build up, a control breaks, an accelerator

163
00:07:17,480 --> 00:07:20,440
stuck on or a brake lost, then division

164
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:24,120
without stopping, a tumour, and, if it's malignant,

165
00:07:24,240 --> 00:07:26,080
invasion and spread.

166
00:07:26,600 --> 00:07:29,000
Next, how common is a disease?

167
00:07:29,240 --> 00:07:31,560
Incidence, prevalence and mortality.

168
00:07:32,767 --> 00:07:34,206
Here are the key notes.

169
00:07:34,447 --> 00:07:36,366
Pause the video if you need more time

170
00:07:36,447 --> 00:07:37,246
to copy them.
